1.18.0: threads and the table - the same pixels, faster

Every image stage now runs in row bands on one thread per core (POSIX
threads; POLAJUICE_THREADS=N overrides, 1 = serial; the wasm build is
serial). A band computes exactly what the serial loop computed for
those rows, in the same order, so output is bit-identical at any
thread count: scripts/test_threads.sh renders at 1, 2 and 5 threads
and compares bytes, and the wasm engine still matches the native CLI
byte for byte. Reductions (the grain field RMS) stay serial.

The sRGB transfer, crossed five or six times per render, goes through
8192-cell interpolated tables instead of powf: error under 2e-5, 8-bit
input decoded exactly. The vertical box-blur pass, half the render
time on a 12 MP frame, now sweeps rows while keeping each column's
running sum and update order; the horizontal pass handles the three
channels in one sweep. Both bit-identical to before. -O3 by default.

Against 1.17.1, a PNG render differs by at most one 8-bit level on a
few thousandths of a percent of samples (the table); tests/ref/ holds
five 1.17.1 renders and test_core requires every camera to reproduce
them within one level on under 0.1% of samples. Table accuracy and
the 8-bit round trip are asserted directly.
